<!--
    MSVC-style two-stage jump tables (Noelo-Lab/kuna#1157): a byte map, one
    byte per tag value, names a row of a table of 4-byte offsets from the
    image base, and the offset table lies directly before its map. The
    first three functions repeat their bounds check on both arms of a
    branch between the check and the dispatch, so the guard analysis finds
    no bound on the map's index.

    Source: fixtures/kuna-bytemap-noguard.asm (nasm -f bin, loaded at
    0x1820e0000); the bytes at 0x182000200 stand for unrelated code of a
    large image. cmp_nodes has two byte-indexed tables, the second reusing
    the index unchecked. cmp_folded is the same, but reads its first map at
    (map - 2) + tag, the normalization folded into the address, so its last
    real row (the 0x33 case) lies past that base. cmp_bytetag indexes a
    16-byte map with a byte tag, so the map's values are proven over all
    256 tags and the bytes after the map count as map values. cmp_coldrow
    reads its 28-row table at (map - 0x61) + c with no bound at all; its
    row 10 lands in a part split off below the entry, as gcc places a
    .cold block, so it lies outside the function's own code but is a real
    row, and the table ends at row 28, the first read from the map.

    Before the fix each first table read row 3 from its map's first four
    bytes, 0x02000200 from the image base, and the flow followed it into the
    code at 0x182000200 (in a 34 MB image, rows like it spread over other
    functions and one decompile ran for 90 seconds). Each table now ends
    where its map begins.

    One pass, no option: a row read from the byte map is false.
-->
